<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jurji Zaydan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jurji Zaidan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jurji Zaydan was a Lebanese novelist, journalist and teacher of the Nahda who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and founded the magazine al-Hilal there in 1892. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> to an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian family, he studied medicine at the Syrian Protestant College before moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His historical novels, about one a year from 1891 and serialised in al-Hilal, aimed to teach ordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> their own history. His secular History of Islamic Civilisation, published from 1901 to 1906, was controversial, and in 1910 public objections cost him a chair at the new Egyptian University.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan.jpg" alt="Jurji Zaydan" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jurji_Zaydan_(Edited2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
